<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kimek Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">743 CE – 1050 CE (approx.) · capital Imakiya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of Turkic tribes on the Irtysh whose western Kipchak wing became the Cumans, masters of the western steppe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kimek confederation formed in the mid-8th century on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh River</a> from tribes, including the Imek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a>, who left the Mongolian steppe as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate</a> rose. By the 9th century their ruler held the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Arab and Persian geographers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gardizi">Gardizi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudud-al-alam">Hudud al-Alam</a> describe the Kimek as a confederation of seven tribes, pastoral but with towns along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a>, with Imakiya as the ruler's seat. They traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and slaves south to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz</a>. Religion included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengrism">Tengrism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaean</a> and Buddhist influences, and they burned their dead.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 11th century the confederation broke up as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a>, its western component, moved west across the steppe. Pressure from the Qai and other peoples of the east also played a part. The Kipchaks became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, who dominated the Pontic steppe until the Mongol conquest.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kimek-khanate.jpg" alt="Kimek Khanate" width="320" height="187"><figcaption>Image: Adaykz · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kimek%E2%80%93Kipchak_confederation.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>743 CE – 1050 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Imakiya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kipchak Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kimek-Kipchak confederation, Kimeks</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=896&amp;focus=kimek-khanate" aria-label="Kimek Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6267 458 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kimek Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6587 714h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6587 714h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Kazakhstan. <a href="/atlas?year=896&amp;focus=kimek-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
